unkId":"lifecycle","kind":"fresh"},{"chunkId":"entrypoints","kind":"fresh"},{"chunkId":"visuals","kind":"fresh"},{"chunkId":"consistency","kind":"fresh"}],"findingDecisions":[{"source":{"attempt":1,"chunkId":"qualification","findingId":"qualification-native-verifier"},"rationale":"Fresh qualification inspection covers the initial toolchain, independent references, inventories and native transformations without mandatory gaps. The previous broad underspecification is narrowed to a demonstrated remaining mismatch in derived Mach-O layout and signature coverage fields.","evidenceReferences":["Checkpoint attempt 2 completedChunks: qualification, limitations=[]","LLD.md:54-56","Accepted qualification-linkedit-derived-variance: independent fs-ext builds have differing __LINKEDIT sizes, string-table sizes, signature offsets and CodeDirectory codeLimit"],"action":"superseded","reviewedInChunkIds":["qualification"],"supersededBy":{"chunkId":"qualification","findingId":"qualification-linkedit-derived-variance"}},{"source":{"attempt":1,"chunkId":"qualification","findingId":"qualification-trust-lifecycle"},"rationale":"Fresh qualification inspection verifies that the revised design defines root-signed trust distribution, scoped qualification keys, freshness and replay floors, admission receipts, expiry/revocation boundaries, offline rollback and durable-success reconciliation. These address the original design omission; concrete shared encoding vectors remain optional implementation advice.","evidenceReferences":["Checkpoint attempt 2 completedChunks: qualification, limitations=[]","LLD.md:49-53","LLD.md:150","LLD.md:194-197","LLD.md:234-238","LLD.md:256-258"],"action":"resolved","reviewedInChunkIds":["qualification"]},{"source":{"attempt":1,"chunkId":"lifecycle","findingId":"lifecycle-rollback-launcher-fence"},"rationale":"Fresh lifecycle inspection verifies that canonical launcher reopening is now delayed until archived restoration and durable rollback publication. The remaining exclusivity defect is the supported predecessor's public update RPC, which can mutate state and schedule shutdown before reaching the guarded dispatcher. The fresh finding captures this residual bypass and required tests.","evidenceReferences":["Checkpoint attempt 2 completedChunks: lifecycle, limitations=[]","LLD.md:79-81","LLD.md:194-197","LLD.md:262","git:ab6a96296ea6f2bde948636424218c0a23136c74:src/daemon/admin-handlers.ts: sharedAdminHandlers and UPDATE_SHUTDOWN_DELAY_MS"],"action":"superseded","reviewedInChunkIds":["lifecycle"],"supersededBy":{"chunkId":"lifecycle","findingId":"lifecycle-legacy-update-rpc-fence"}},{"source":{"attempt":1,"chunkId":"entrypoints","findingId":"entrypoints-bootstrap-before-verification"},"rationale":"Fresh entrypoint inspection verifies an explicit replacement contract: the trusted installer and IDE distributions carry an independent verifier/root and authenticate the exact bridge source before checkout, build scripts or bridge execution. Controller-boundary negative authorization tests are specified. This resolves the design omission without claiming the existing implementation already satisfies it.","evidenceReferences":["Checkpoint attempt 2 completedChunks: entrypoints, limitations=[]","LLD.md:49-50","LLD.md:234-238","LLD.md:257","Canonical /body/contractDetails/api/0/preconditions"],"action":"resolved","reviewedInChunkIds":["entrypoints"]},{"source":{"attempt":1,"chunkId":"visuals","findingId":"visuals-relative-companion-link"},"rationale":"The live document still repeats its repository-relative directory in the companion link, and the document-relative target is absent. Carry the original defect. The fresh visuals chunk also retains a mandatory rendering gap, so it cannot support a completed resolution disposition.","evidenceReferences":["LLD.md:160","Canonical /body/companions/0/relPath","Filesystem check: nested document-relative target absent; repository-relative sequence-diagram.html exists","Checkpoint attempt 2 completedChunks: visuals, visuals-current-relative-link and rendering limitation"],"action":"carry"}],"effectiveFindingOrigins":[{"attempt":1,"chunkId":"visuals","findingId":"visuals-relative-companion-link"},{"attempt":2,"chunkId":"qualification","findingId":"qualification-linkedit-derived-variance"},{"attempt":2,"chunkId":"lifecycle","findingId":"lifecycle-legacy-update-rpc-fence"},{"attempt":2,"chunkId":"entrypoints","findingId":"entrypoints-managed-preparation-ack-order"},{"attempt":2,"chunkId":"visuals","findingId":"visuals-current-relative-link"}]}},"finishedAt":"2026-11-12T20:02:58.166Z"},{"number":3,"status":"incomplete","preparationKind":"impact","baseline":{"subjectFingerprints":{"canonical":"637076594e2ad0abdca040fc1d443d512c28b19301adbce8843191b2884a30a8","document":"0b38e07b5ce9469c0c535b55fee08a81703aaee55a24bf1e4830e96fcb8f70c7"},"contentIdentity":"083b5fdeb0733343496063af4a9d07441402ecd17e5e777ff9905350d49a4d63","reviewedContent":{"canonical":"{\n  \"meta\": {\n    \"workflow\": \"design.story\",\n    \"runId\": \"wf-1794513859350-cd601e\",\n    \"repoPath\": \"/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc\",\n    \"createdAt\": \"2026-11-12T20:04:20.657Z\",\n    \"epicCreatedAt\": \"2026-11-11T16:00:50.440Z\",\n    \"attribution\": {\n      \"outputs\": [\n        {\n          \"role\": \"(client)\",\n          \"tier\": \"core\",\n          \"runner\": \"cli-codex\",\n          \"model\": \"unknown\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"elapsedMs\": 925,\n    \"repoIndexedAt\": null,\n    \"schemaVersion\": 1,\n    \"epicHash\": \"5d5478c61a25ac8f\",\n    \"epicSlug\": \"enable-managed-daemon-lifecycle-macos-install\",\n    \"storyId\": \"s1\",\n    \"hldBaseRunId\": \"wf-1794513859350-cd601e\",\n    \"hldEffectiveHash\": \"c1ae66d7690a2a8a934ca19f95afb661df8e1cd4f2f14043b60e3bd9f8e6b625\",\n    \"hldAmendmentsApplied\": [],\n    \"storyTitle\": \"Enable managed daemon lifecycle on macOS install and upgrade\",\n    \"standalone\": true,\n    \"authoredBy\": \"controller\",\n    \"lifecycle\": {\n      \"status\": \"draft\",\n      \"revision\": {\n        \"number\": 5,\n        \"contentHash\": \"8ea618f95ebc498c344e3428f69790f86ab39341cbcb419ce6516a67942a35cb\",\n        \"at\": \"2026-11-12T20:04:20.768Z\",\n        \"by\": {\n          \"id\": \"insrc:system\",\n          \"kind\": \"system\",\n          \"properties\": {}\n        }\n      },\n      \"signoffs\": []\n    },\n    \"envelope\": {\n      \"attachments\": []\n    },\n    \"revision\": 5\n  },\n  \"body\": {\n    \"hldContextSlice\": {\n      \"frameworkSummary\": \"Standalone feature — no parent HLD. Design directly against the repo, grounded on the s1 analyze passes. There are no HLD shared contracts to honour.\",\n      \"ownedContracts\": [],\n      \"consumedContracts\": [],\n      \"boundary\": {\n        \"storyId\": \"s1\",\n        \"owns\": [],\n        \"depends\": [],\n        \"internal\": \"Enable managed daemon lifecycle on macOS install and upgrade\"\n      },\n      \"adjacentBoundaries\": [],\n      \"rolloutPhase\": \"standalone\",\n      \"nonFunctional\": {}\n    },\n    \"summary\": {\n      \"audience\": \"product\",\n      \"prose\": \"The normal macOS installer provisions a per-user supervisor and automatically migrates supported locally built installs. Before cutover the updater verifies the incumbent against release-authorized build evidence and pins its original bytes for rollback. Existing configuration, stores and running state survive a verified rollback.\"\n    },\n    \"contractDetails\": {\n      \"surfaceLevel\": \"internal-shared\",\n      \"api\": [\n        {\n          \"name\": \"stageManagedRelease\",\n          \"signature\": \"stageManagedRelease(options: StageOptions): Promise<{ readonly candidate: StagedReleasePin; readonly prior?: StagedReleasePin; readonly compatibility?: QualifiedReleasePair }>\",\n          \"parameters\": [\n            {\n              \"name\": \"options\",\n              \"type\": \"StageOptions\",\n              \"purpose\": \"Carries the configured source, exact release pair, authorized build evidence and optional incumbent.\",\n              \"optional\": false\n            }\n          ],\n          \"returns\": {\n            \"type\": \"Promise<{ readonly candidate: StagedReleasePin; readonly prior?: StagedReleasePin; readonly compatibility?: QualifiedReleasePair }>\",\n            \"meaning\": \"Verified candidate and optional byte-exact original rollback pin.\"\n          },\n          \"errors\": [\n            {\n              \"type\": \"Error\",\n              \"condition\": \"Missing or invalid signed evidence, unsupported build profile, unaccounted executable differences, ambiguous inventory or unstable pin.\"\n            }\n          ],\n          \"preconditions\": [\n            \"The official installer script and IDE-bundled copies carry the same dependency-free Node verifier and pinned offline release root. An installed managed controller can reuse its verified copy. Fetch into a fresh owner-private bare Git directory with external hooks, checkout filters and config injection disabled. Before checkout, npm scripts, build, or launch of a downloaded bridge, the trusted verifier validates the signed source/branch/full commit and expected Git tree, approved build inputs and release policy. A configured URL override changes routing only. Never obtain the first verifier or root from the fetched branch.\",\n            \"Use separately root-signed UTF-8 trust data, and per-channel Ed25519 qualification keys authorized by that data. Verify signatures over exact length-framed UTF-8 payload bytes with distinct trust and qualification domain tags. Enforce strict versioned schemas and limits and reject duplicate keys. Trust data records monotonic epoch, permitted source and branch per key, key ID and public key, validity window and revocations. The root is pinned in the official distribution. Root rotation requires an updated trusted distribution. Production verifier trust excludes fixture keys.\"\n          ],\n          \"postconditions\": [\n            \"Refresh root-signed trust data from the configured release channel during preparation and within five minutes before the first cutover mutation. Trust data is valid for at most 24 hours. Validate the UTC window, key status and epoch against an owner-private durable floor advanced atomically under admission. If the same epoch arrives with different bytes, the local clock has rolled behind the stored valid-time floor, or signed trust data is unavailable, refuse admission. Qualification carries key ID, release source, exact current branch head and Git tree, ordered release pair, validity window, supported profile and immutable release-test receipt digests. A revoked key, expired qualification, stale trust epoch or changed candidate prevents cutover.\",\n            \"Initial profile targets the measured arm64 macOS 27.0 installation class and predecessor ab6a96296ea6f2bde948636424218c0a23136c74. Reference inputs pin Node 22.23.2 with ABI 127, npm 10.9.8, node-gyp 11.5.0, Node 22.22.1 headers, Apple Clang 21.0.0 build 2100.3.34.2, Command Line Tools 27.0.0.0.1788430756, SDK 27.0, Python 3.12.6, -std=c++17 and exact Git tree and lockfile. Release authority records digests for tools, SDK/headers, dependencies, prebuilds and environment allowlist. It produces references from two clean installer builds at separate real paths, with pinned inputs and controlled network dependency retrieval. Each supported optional dependency layout is inventoried and qualifies separately. No profile ships before the real product matrix confirms these inputs and the target incumbent.\",\n            \"Inventory exact relative paths, type, permitted modes, symlink targets, and digests for the Node runtime, the executable closure in out and node_modules, project-memory dist, scripts used by the daemon or supported entry points, and build/package configuration used by those entry points. Walk by stable owned file handles without following unapproved links. Reject extra or missing execution-bearing files, escaped links or ambiguous optional package layouts. Default to raw SHA-256. A signed profile may explicitly enumerate inert build leftovers with their expected formats and paths. Generated Makefile, gyp and dependency records, intermediate Mach-O object/static library files and npm bookkeeping are eligible only if a verified runtime or rollback path never reads or executes them. Validate their types and allowlisted formats and retain every byte in the raw pin. Optional JavaScript or WASM packages are executable and use a separately qualified exact inventory.\",\n            \"Native equivalence is limited initially to the signed exact paths of the fs-ext, tree-sitter, tree-sitter-sql and tree-sitter-kotlin thin arm64 bundles observed in the native study. Parse the entire file without gaps or overlaps. Preserve raw bytes of code, mapped data, all padding outside explicitly validated linker regions, and loader semantics including virtual addresses, dependency names, binding, export, rebase, relocation and indirect-symbol tables. Only known debug-only N_SO and N_OSO records may replace an exact observed checkout root with a token. Only N_OSO may normalize its object timestamp. Preserve record order, indices, types, sections, flags, all other values and decoded names. Verify runtime tables never reference any normalized debug record or a mutable byte of its strings. Reject all other native variations.\",\n            \"Derive the normalized representation from a checked physical layout. For each profile require the signed region order, alignment, permitted padding bytes and extent rules. Verify LC_SYMTAB addresses and sizes select its original bounded symbol and string tables. Decode and validate all n_strx references and account for the complete original string table including suffix sharing, terminators and padding. Preserve exact non-debug values and all runtime reference relationships. Construct a deterministic tokenized string table from the ordered symbol records and retained non-debug strings, with stable offsets assigned in first-appearance order. Replace only validated physical offsets, lengths and string offsets with logical region identifiers and their derived canonical offsets and lengths. Compute the normalized LC_SYMTAB strsize and __LINKEDIT filesize from these canonical extents while preserving loader-visible addresses, protections and alignments. Verify LC_CODE_SIGNATURE dataoff equals the validated original signature boundary and original CodeDirectory codeLimit equals dataoff, with complete coverage from offset zero and no unsigned gap or trailing payload. Verify the original linker-signed ad hoc CodeDirectory page hashes, expected identity, flags and absence of extra slots or entitlements. Represent signature offset and codeLimit by the same derived canonical boundary after normalization and preserve the verified signature policy in the digest. Normalize the linker UUID and generated page hashes only after those checks. Compare the versioned logical representation with the signed reference. The representation is evidence only and is never executed or written over the original binary.\",\n            \"After independent verification capture and retain a byte-exact digest of every installed runtime and allowed inert file, including portable verifier metadata. Journal the raw incumbent and candidate pins and the signed evidence digest with the exact update before fencing. Recheck raw bytes and identities on both sides of the fence and before rollback. Build the authenticated candidate with the qualified inputs, and verify its signed inventory before executing its installed controller or daemon.\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"name\": \"completeLegacyMigration\",\n          \"signature\": \"completeLegacyMigration(gate: InstallationMutationGate, update: UpdateReservation, pins: ManagedUpdatePins): Promise<DaemonUpdateOutcome>\",\n          \"parameters\": [\n            {\n              \"name\": \"gate\",\n              \"type\": \"InstallationMutationGate\",\n              \"purpose\": \"Owns the cutover and recovery reservation.\",\n              \"optional\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"update\",\n              \"type\": \"UpdateReservation\",\n              \"purpose\": \"Identifies verified predecessor, release, fence and desired state.\",\n              \"optional\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"pins\",\n              \"type\": \"ManagedUpdatePins\",\n              \"purpose\": \"Carries the qualified prior and candidate releases.\",\n              \"optional\": false\n            }\n          ],\n          \"returns\": {\n            \"type\": \"Promise<DaemonUpdateOutcome>\",\n            \"meaning\": \"Verified committed cutover, verified rollback, or fenced recovery.\"\n          },\n          \"errors\": [\n            {\n              \"type\": \"SupervisorLifecycleError\",\n              \"condition\": \"A release, launch route, exact exit, or recovery state cannot be verified.\"\n            }\n          ],\n          \"preconditions\": [\n            \"Use the migration-capable installer or updater to prepare the release before invoking any shutdown path. Freeze the canonical legacy launch and checkout paths and verify old mutating helpers are absent before cutover. Verify the pinned predecessor and public socket before sending shutdown.\"\n          ],\n          \"postconditions\": [\n            \"Preserve the original raw pin, job policy and stopped or running intent. Keep the canonical dispatcher fenced during candidate cleanup, selector reconciliation, predecessor launch and readiness verification, and until the rollback outcome and receipt are durable.\",\n            \"For a prior running install, the reservation owner alone may use the private, byte-pinned archived checkout. Reconstruct direct argv and cwd or a private launchd descriptor, mapping only pre-recorded checkout-contained executable/script/cwd paths from the captured inode to its archived location. Preserve UID, domain, label, argument order, runtime, state-root environment and RunAtLoad/KeepAlive policy. Verify the actual registered job and socket peer before recording readiness. A previously stopped job stays passive. Refuse ambiguous mapping and retain the fence.\",\n            \"Publish the terminal rollback outcome and verified legacy route together before enabling normal access. Keep a guarded canonical dispatcher after rollback. It can select the archived release only for the verified terminal receipt, route restart and update through the same gate, and support a later managed retry without releasing the old mutating scripts. Keep the archived location stable for the live job. Journal dispatch publication separately so interrupted settlement can reconcile without another spawn. Verify exact child or idle state and preserve a committed candidate without rollback.\",\n            \"Every restored running legacy route uses a trusted, byte-pinned rollback preload from the current installer/controller, passed as an explicit Node --import argument after scrubbing user injection options. For the signed predecessor profile the synchronous Node module hook matches the verified archived admin-handlers module URL and the pinned main/admin-worker importer URLs. It supplies wrappers for both sharedAdminHandlers and adminWorkerHandlers, retaining the original table except daemon.update. The wrapper returns an update-via-current-installer refusal before invoking any old handler, so it never writes the old marker, spawns the old helper or schedules shutdown. The restored daemon keeps this refusal after terminal rollback and throughout any later migration attempt. The runtime and archived executable keep their raw pins. Set the launcher root to the stable guarded canonical dispatcher, while the daemon entry and cwd use the verified archive. Never resolve launcher operations through the archived checkout.\",\n            \"At startup require the preload and exact hook configuration in the main thread and any admin worker, including replacements. For the pinned old router, queued admin requests can only reach a constructed wrapped table. Profile validation proves the supported startup and worker imports, default worker argument inheritance and absence of an unguarded update route. A private preload receipt identifies the verified module and invocation for main and the admin worker. Fail startup or keep recovery fenced if the wrapper cannot attest either applicable path before readiness acceptance. Test both threaded and single-threaded operation. The original legacy IPC is unavailable for updating a restored predecessor. Current shell, TUI and IDE paths use the trusted installer route.\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"name\": \"stageManagedInstallation\",\n          \"signature\": \"stageManagedInstallation(installation: ManagedInstallation, revalidate: () => Promise<void>, legacyCutover?: boolean): Promise<void>\",\n          \"parameters\": [\n            {\n              \"name\": \"installation\",\n              \"type\": \"ManagedInstallation\",\n              \"purpose\": \"Identifies the owned state root, release, runtime and selector.\",\n              \"optional\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"revalidate\",\n              \"type\": \"() => Promise<void>\",\n              \"purpose\": \"Verifies transaction ownership before a write.\",\n              \"optional\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"legacyCutover\",\n              \"type\": \"boolean\",\n              \"purpose\": \"Selects staging for a verified predecessor.\",\n              \"optional\": true\n            }\n          ],\n          \"returns\": {\n            \"type\": \"Promise<void>\",\n            \"meaning\": \"Writes the staged disabled selector and its descriptor only.\"\n          },\n          \"errors\": [\n            {\n              \"type\": \"SupervisorLifecycleError\",\n              \"condition\": \"Selector, descriptor, or state root conflicts with the reservation.\"\n            }\n          ],\n          \"preconditions\": [\n            \"The installer controller owns an activation reservation with the explicit start intent and candidate pin. It has verified the canonical root, GUI launchd domain and quiescent fresh state.\"\n          ],\n          \"postconditions\": [\n            \"The installer controller journals descriptor and provisional-selector publication, exact job registration and idle handshake, and optional child readiness. Enable the selector under the reservation before controller admission. Release the reservation only after a verified idle or running result is durable. --no-start leaves an enabled selector and an idle supervisor. Recovery replays an exact matching partial pair or removes only artifacts created by the activation and restores prior absence.\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"name\": \"launchManagedUpdate\",\n          \"signature\": \"launchManagedUpdate(entry: string, args: readonly string[], awaitReply: () => Promise<boolean>): Promise<{ launched: true; updateId: string }>\",\n          \"parameters\": [\n            {\n              \"name\": \"entry\",\n              \"type\": \"string\",\n              \"purpose\": \"Pins the detached controller executable.\",\n              \"optional\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"args\",\n              \"type\": \"readonly string[]\",\n              \"purpose\": \"Carries the owned release and operation inputs.\",\n              \"optional\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"awaitReply\",\n              \"type\": \"() => Promise<boolean>\",\n              \"purpose\": \"Confirms that the update acknowledgement was written.\",\n              \"optional\": false\n            }\n          ],\n          \"returns\": {\n            \"type\": \"Promise<{ launched: true; updateId: string }>\",\n            \"meaning\": \"Returns the admitted update identity while the helper owns completion.\"\n          },\n          \"errors\": [\n            {\n              \"type\": \"SupervisorLifecycleError\",\n              \"condition\": \"An update is already active or the helper cannot qualify the candidate.\"\n            }\n          ],\n          \"preconditions\": [\n            \"The already trusted installed helper reserves the update and returns its acknowledgement within the existing client deadline. Launch it without fetching, installing, building or inventory verification before the acknowledgement. Stopped updates use the local controller and preserve idle intent.\"\n          ],\n          \"postconditions\": [\n            \"Begin detached preparation only after confirmation that the acknowledgement was written. If reply delivery fails, cancel the unstarted reservation and leave the incumbent running. The helper verifies qualification and stages the candidate before any launcher fence, shutdown or selector change. A lost client connection after confirmed reply does not cancel the admitted update. Preserve the existing response shape and the test for preparation longer than both transport deadlines.\"\n          ]\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"dataModelChanges\": [\n      {\n        \"entity\": \"InstallationMutationGate activation reservation\",\n        \"change\": \"field-add\",\n        \"details\": \"Persist one activation reservation per state root with exact owner, candidate and explicit start intent. Journal descriptor, provisional selector, exact registration, child admission, readiness and terminal outcome with identities. Previous journals remain decodable. Activation cleanup reverses only receipted owned mutations.\",\n        \"callSites\": [\n          \"src/shared/installation-mutation-gate.ts\",\n          \"src/shared/managed-daemon-installation.ts\",\n          \"src/bin/insrc-daemon-control.ts\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"entity\": \"UpdatePredecessor\",\n        \"change\": \"invariant-change\",\n        \"details\": \"Persist a tagged managed, legacy-job, legacy-direct, or legacy-stopped predecessor. All variants bind canonical state root, prior release/runtime and candidate pin, selector baseline or absence, and prior running state. Managed carries supervisor generation. Legacy-job carries descriptor path/hash, verified label, UID, launchd domain, program argv, working directory and RunAtLoad policy. Running legacy-job and legacy-direct carry exact process incarnation, authenticated socket peer and pinned PID/socket identities. Legacy-direct also carries rollback argv, cwd and a release-qualified environment recipe reconstructed from the supported launcher, without persisting credentials. Legacy-stopped carries proof of no live process and either a pinned idle job or verified absence. Decode historical predecessor.legacy as legacy-job and older enabled prior without legacy as managed. Reject unknown or contradictory variants. Persist each observed exit, its drain classification, and ownership-fence evidence before candidate bootstrap. A rollback route maps only verified original checkout-relative argv/cwd paths into the archived directory. It binds a single archived release and recorded job or direct launch to the update and keeps the canonical launcher fenced until terminal evidence. The archive route pins one trusted preload, its supported old release and import/worker topology. The stable launcher root is distinct from the archived daemon cwd and entry. The old update RPC remains refused for this restoration generation.\",\n        \"callSites\": [\n          \"src/shared/installation-mutation-gate.ts\",\n          \"src/daemon/managed-update-recovery.ts\",\n          \"src/daemon/legacy-launch-job.ts\",\n          \"src/daemon/legacy-daemon-cutover.ts\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"entity\": \"Release qualification profile and exact rollback pin\",\n        \"change\": \"invariant-change\",\n        \"details\": \"The release authority owns a signed, versioned profile for one configured source, ordered commit pair and tested build profile. A release pair can have multiple profiles. Exactly one applicable profile must verify a particular installation. An update reservation owns one verified profile receipt and one exact incumbent pin and binds both to its candidate, selector and state root. The profile verifies a complete measured inventory and accounts for variability. The local pin freezes raw bytes for rollback, and the receipt is reusable during recovery without remote network access. Historical journals retain their original verification rules. Versioned notes that lack the required portable proof cannot qualify a source-built variant. Record one immutable admission receipt per update with signing key, trust epoch and payload digest, verification time, qualification and reference digests, commit/tree pair, source, release profile, raw pins and exact update ID. Before a recorded fence, expiry, refresh failure or revocation forces refusal. After the fence, the recorded decision applies only to that transaction while its owner settles it within its admitted authorization window. If recovery begins without a durable success, it can verify and roll back offline using the receipt and raw pin. It needs fresh authorization to newly activate a candidate. If revocation is known before a new candidate activation, roll back. Durable success is reconciled offline and is never reinterpreted as uncommitted.\",\n        \"callSites\": [\n          \"src/daemon/managed-release-staging.ts\",\n          \"src/daemon/managed-update-preparation.ts\",\n          \"src/daemon/release-compatibility.ts\",\n          \"src/daemon/managed-update-recovery.ts\"\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"interactionWithShared\": [],\n    \"errorPaths\": {\n      \"errorCases\": [\n        {\n          \"scenario\": \"An older launcher or updater can still mutate the legacy install\",\n          \"detection\": \"Resolve the canonical checkout and every supported launch route. Atomically exchange the canonical checkout entry with a gate-aware dispatcher on the same filesystem while the old daemon is still running. Preserve the displaced checkout separately. Before stopping, verify the dispatcher and archived checkout identities, an absent or owned legacy update marker, and absence of known old shell, detached helper and installer processes bound to the retired root, using verified argv and cwd. Repeat admission after the swap. An unrecognized launch path, unresolved process identity, live old mutator, or exchange failure makes exclusivity unproven.\",\n          \"response\": \"The dispatcher rejects concurrent mutation while cutover owns the reservation. Qualify only predecessors that acquire the shared run-file guard before opening data and whose shipped old commands use the canonical absolute entry. Keep that guard through owned cleanup and transfer it to candidate startup before the legacy route can reacquire it. Refuse when another legacy route cannot be fenced. Preserve or restore the old service before releasing a failed admission.\",\n          \"userImpact\": \"The supported update retries safely after a concurrent operation ends.\",\n          \"recoverable\": true\n        },\n        {\n          \"scenario\": \"Predecessor job or runtime does not match the qualified route\",\n          \"detection\": \"Match the verified launchd registration against its owned descriptor bytes and approved exact argv. Match direct socket peer and executable against the qualified launch recipe. Accept the documented recovery LaunchAgent only with its pinned tsx loader arguments, canonical entry, matching working directory, bounded owned descriptor and passive restart policy.\",\n          \"response\": \"Reject foreign jobs, unknown loaders or args, conflicting restart policy and unverifiable direct rollback settings before disruption. Recheck the descriptor, PID incarnation and run-file identities immediately before each action.\",\n          \"userImpact\": \"The updater reports the unsupported predecessor while preserving the current service.\",\n          \"recoverable\": true\n        },\n        {\n          \"scenario\": \"Exit is unconfirmed or graceful drain cannot be classified\",\n          \"detection\": \"Observe the exact predecessor exit independently of the broker and bind any qualified terminal evidence to the authenticated shutdown request and process incarnation. An RPC acknowledgement, socket close, PID disappearance, or generic log entry alone does not prove a graceful drain.\",\n          \"response\": \"Withhold candidate admission until exact exit is proved. Journal the exit plus graceful, broker-denied, or unknown. Use graceful only for a qualified positive completion signal, broker-denied only for correlated watchdog evidence, and unknown otherwise. Both broker-denied and unknown require a release pair explicitly qualified for crash-consistent reopening. Report the observed classification in the update detail.\",\n          \"userImpact\": \"The user can distinguish a verified shutdown from an exit whose drain could not be confirmed.\",\n          \"recoverable\": true\n        },\n        {\n          \"scenario\": \"Candidate bootstrap or readiness fails before a durable outcome\",\n          \"detection\": \"Registration, startup generation, owned run files, or candidate idle/readiness cannot be verified.\",\n          \"response\": \"Reconcile the candidate under the reservation, Keep the canonical dispatcher fenced while restoring the predecessor via the archived route. Publish verified rollback before enabling guarded ordinary commands. Retain a fence when restoration is unverified.\",\n          \"userImpact\": \"Verified rollback restores the previous service state and reports the failed update.\",\n          \"recoverable\": true\n        },\n        {\n          \"scenario\": \"Outcome publication throws or its acknowledgement is lost\",\n          \"detection\": \"Publication fails, or retry sees an active reservation without a verified terminal reply.\",\n          \"response\": \"Keep the reservation. Re-read the exact matching durable outcome through existing recovery. A durable success verifies or completes activation without rollback. A durable rollback verifies the old state. Only a proven uncommitted transaction can unwind. Unknown ownership remains fenced.\",\n          \"userImpact\": \"The updater reports an unconfirmed result until recovery determines the terminal state.\",\n          \"recoverable\": true\n        },\n        {\n          \"scenario\": \"Release qualification is missing, ambiguous, stale, revoked or invalid\",\n          \"detection\": \"Reject absent note, invalid signature or key, unsupported profile or verifier version, source or commit mismatch, conflicting profiles and any unaccounted runtime difference.\",\n          \"response\": \"Report the failed verification before any incumbent shutdown, fence or selector mutation. Discard only owned preparation artifacts and preserve the incumbent.\",\n          \"userImpact\": \"The daemon remains available with an update diagnostic and can retry after a qualified release is published.\",\n          \"recoverable\": true\n        },\n        {\n          \"scenario\": \"Installed runtime changes after independent verification\",\n          \"detection\": \"Recheck the original raw digest and exact file identities while holding update admission at the designated cutover and rollback boundaries.\",\n          \"response\": \"Stop cutover before touching the incumbent when possible. After mutation use the existing exact rollback or fenced recovery. Refuse to execute any changed rollback copy.\",\n          \"userImpact\": \"The update fails with a verification diagnostic.\",\n          \"recoverable\": true\n        },\n        {\n          \"scenario\": \"Legacy mutation competes with rollback or cutover authorization expires\",\n          \"detection\": \"Read the admitted trust receipt and launcher fence identities. Detect a concurrent old command through the guarded canonical route or a fresh candidate admission after expiry or revocation.\",\n          \"response\": \"Reject the old command. Use only the private archive route for rollback, verify the prior state, publish terminal evidence, then permit guarded dispatch. If admission is no longer valid, recover offline by rollback unless the candidate already has a verified durable success.\",\n          \"userImpact\": \"The previous verified service is restored or the installation remains fenced for recovery.\",\n          \"recoverable\": true\n        },\n        {\n          \"scenario\": \"A restored legacy client attempts daemon.update or the rollback preload cannot bind the old handler\",\n          \"detection\": \"The pinned adapter wraps both legacy admin table factories and records the receipt for each applicable execution path. Refuse before the original RPC handler. Reject a startup without proven coverage.\",\n          \"response\": \"Keep the legacy update RPC disabled before and after settlement. Use the guarded current installer path for a subsequent update. Retain fenced recovery if no verified restored service can be admitted.\",\n          \"userImpact\": \"The previous service remains available through supported commands or the controller reports fenced recovery.\",\n          \"recoverable\": true\n        }\n      ],\n      \"edgeCases\": [\n        {\n          \"input\": \"Fresh --no-start\",\n          \"expected\": \"Register the supervisor, enable the selector and verify idle with no daemon child. Subsequent normal start uses the same supervisor.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"input\": \"Already-managed update when the daemon is stopped\",\n          \"expected\": \"The external controller prepares and commits the update using supervisor status; no daemon RPC is required. Keep the candidate idle.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"input\": \"Legacy daemon remains running while the candidate is staged\",\n          \"expected\": \"Build outside the live root, preserve the running predecessor and only then acquire a cutover reservation. Failed fetch, build, or qualification leaves the running install unchanged.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"input\": \"An unchanged older shell or IDE freshness client invokes legacy restart or daemon.update\",\n          \"expected\": \"An old client cannot acquire new preflight behavior before executing its existing shutdown sequence. The first upgrade is delivered by the current standard installer or the ordinary update action in updated IDE clients. The installer may be rerun on an existing installation without launchctl or file edits. Never present an old acknowledgement as migration success.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"input\": \"Two qualified installs share a commit but have different build paths or raw native artifact bytes\",\n          \"expected\": \"Each matches independent expected execution-bearing bytes under the same tested profile and retains its own raw exact pin.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"invariantsToPreserve\": [\n        {\n          \"text\": \"One state root has at most one daemon process admitted to use its stores.\",\n          \"source\": \"c2\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"text\": \"Committed success is never rolled back because the publication reply was lost.\",\n          \"source\": \"c4\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"text\": \"Linux keeps its current lifecycle and update behavior.\",\n          \"source\": \"c1\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"testStrategy\": {\n      \"testLevels\": [\n        {\n          \"level\": \"unit\",\n          \"purpose\": \"Verify admission, qualification, journal compatibility and outcome recovery.\",\n          \"subjects\": [\n            \"Predecessor variants and backward decoding\",\n            \"Explicit fresh activation start intent and owned partial publication\",\n            \"Release provenance, changed bytes and absent legacy declaration\",\n            \"Graceful, broker-denied and unknown drain evidence\",\n            \"Uncertain publication with durable success versus no outcome\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"level\": \"integration\",\n          \"purpose\": \"Drive supported entry points and competing legacy operations through the real controller boundaries.\",\n          \"subjects\": [\n            \"Installer and updated CLI, VS Code and JetBrains route matrix\",\n            \"Running managed update IPC and stopped local managed update\",\n            \"Old shell/helper concurrent with canonical launcher exchange and old child attempting to open stores\",\n            \"Startup guard transfer, candidate bootstrap failure, rollback and repeated recovery\",\n            \"Linux normal install, update and restart unchanged\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"level\": \"smoke\",\n          \"purpose\": \"Verify actual launchd and daemon state on macOS.\",\n          \"subjects\": [\n            \"Fresh install and verified ordinary restart\",\n            \"Fresh --no-start and later supervisor start\",\n            \"First supported update of a qualified prior build with broker denial\",\n            \"Direct install and passive recovery job with exact loader argv\",\n            \"Verified stopped and running rollback with configuration and store integrity\"\n          ],\n          \"fixturesNeeded\": [\n            \"Disposable macOS state roots and isolated launchd labels\",\n            \"Pinned released predecessors, including the regression and clean-drain cases\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"level\": \"contract\",\n          \"purpose\": \"Verify release signatures, independent expected inventory, applicability, exact runtime coverage, and report provenance.\",\n          \"subjects\": [\n            \"Independent same-commit macOS build equivalence and tamper rejection\",\n            \"Changed native code, loadable data, bindings, signature policy, injected modules, symlinks and unidentified variance fail closed\",\n            \"Missing or stale release evidence prevents promotion and leaves the incumbent running\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"level\": \"smoke\",\n          \"purpose\": \"Require real product builds before promoting a source-built profile.\",\n          \"subjects\": [\n            \"Release publication validates real running and stopped upgrades for every enabled profile\",\n            \"Real product passive and recovery job loader arguments, verified broker refusal, data-preserving rollback and subsequent managed update\"\n          ],\n          \"fixturesNeeded\": [\n            \"Two independent production installer builds of the same supported predecessor in different homes with observed native variance\",\n            \"Real predecessor and candidate installs using disposable homes and distinct launchd jobs\",\n            \"Qualification from an isolated release authority, plus immutable test receipts bound to promoted release metadata\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"level\": \"integration\",\n          \"purpose\": \"Prove trust ordering and rollback fences at the real controller boundaries.\",\n          \"subjects\": [\n            \"Trust rotation, expiry, replay and offline rollback at the fence boundary\",\n            \"Invalid or missing authorization prevents bootstrap checkout execution, npm scripts and bridge invocation\",\n            \"A competing legacy command and interruption during rollback cannot reopen the launcher before durable outcome\"\n          ],\n          \"fixturesNeeded\": [\n            \"A trusted installer and distinct trusted/unauthorized signed Git origins with executable marker probes\",\n            \"A real archived legacy daemon, fresh and expired signed trust epochs, and controller crash points before outcome and before guarded dispatch\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"level\": \"contract\",\n          \"purpose\": \"Cover derived Mach-O layout, signature coverage and preserved runtime semantics.\",\n          \"subjects\": [\n            \"Independent unequal-length debug roots preserve canonical native identity and malformed offsets or codeLimit are rejected\"\n          ],\n          \"fixturesNeeded\": [\n            \"Two real fs-ext builds with different path lengths and different physical string-table size, __LINKEDIT extent, signature offset and codeLimit\",\n            \"Malformed linkedit offsets, unexpected string aliases or padding, truncated CodeDirectory and mismatched codeLimit\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"level\": \"integration\",\n          \"purpose\": \"Prove restored old IPC containment and preserve the bounded managed acknowledgement.\",\n          \"subjects\": [\n            \"Real restored legacy update RPC cannot mutate the marker, run the old helper or stop the daemon during rollback and managed retry\",\n            \"Managed acknowledgement precedes a preparation exceeding the client deadlines and lost reply cancels only unstarted work\"\n          ],\n          \"fixturesNeeded\": [\n            \"Actual old predecessor with normal and threaded admin routing and worker replacement, paused before rollback publication and during a later managed preparation\",\n            \"Existing managed-update-handoff slow preparation fixture, Unix socket delivery failure and correlated update outcome\"\n          ]\n        }\n      ],\n      \"acceptanceMapping\": [\n        {\n          \"criterionId\": \"ac1\",\n          \"provingTests\": [\n            \"Fresh install and verified ordinary restart\",\n            \"Fresh --no-start and later supervisor start\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"criterionId\": \"ac2\",\n          \"provingTests\": [\n            \"Installer and updated CLI, VS Code and JetBrains route matrix\",\n            \"First supported update of a qualified prior build with broker denial\",\n            \"Direct install and passive recovery job with exact loader argv\",\n            \"Old shell/helper concurrent with canonical launcher exchange and old child attempting to open stores\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"criterionId\": \"ac3\",\n          \"provingTests\": [\n            \"Running managed update IPC and stopped local managed update\",\n            \"Verified stopped and running rollback with configuration and store integrity\",\n            \"Uncertain publication with durable success versus no outcome\",\n            \"Linux normal install, update and restart unchanged\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"criterionId\": \"ac4\",\n          \"provingTests\": [\n            \"Independent same-commit macOS build equivalence and tamper rejection\",\n            \"Release publication validates real running and stopped upgrades for every enabled profile\",\n            \"Trust rotation, expiry, replay and offline rollback at the fence boundary\",\n            \"Invalid or missing authorization prevents bootstrap checkout execution, npm scripts and bridge invocation\",\n            \"A competing legacy command and interruption during rollback cannot reopen the launcher before durable outcome\",\n            \"Independent unequal-length debug roots preserve canonical native identity and malformed offsets or codeLimit are rejected\",\n            \"Real restored legacy update RPC cannot mutate the marker, run the old helper or stop the daemon during rollback and managed retry\",\n            \"Managed acknowledgement precedes a preparation exceeding the client deadlines and lost reply cancels only unstarted work\"\n          ]\n        }\n      ],\n      \"testFramework\": \"Node.js test runner via tsx, installed shell integration fixtures, and real macOS launchd smoke tests in disposable state roots.\"\n    },\n    \"migration\": {\n      \"stateBefore\": \"The s1 discovery bundle identified whole-tree prior fingerprint qualification before managed migration. Two builds of the same legacy commit produced different native executable bytes. The configured origin was missing the qualification ref.\",\n      \"stateAfter\": \"Current install and update entry points share a migration-capable controller and immutable releases. A supported first upgrade retains the previous release and establishes one verified per-user supervisor. User data remains in the existing state root.\",\n      \"migrationSteps\": [\n        {\n          \"order\": 1,\n          \"action\": \"The release maintainer builds independent references, signs profile and product test receipt digests, and uploads qualification for the exact target commit. A promotion gate verifies the note and reference receipts against the intended head before advancing the channel ref. Publish the note before the branch moves, or publish both atomically. Re-fetch both from the ordinary updater endpoint and confirm the signed head and trust epoch agree. Unsupported profiles remain unavailable. Fixture keys never authorize production.\",\n          \"rollbackable\": true\n        },\n        {\n          \"order\": 2,\n          \"action\": \"Use the current standard installer for both fresh installs and first upgrades. Updated VS Code and JetBrains lifecycle Update on unmanaged Darwin dispatch the packaged migration-capable installer in existing-install mode with the configured branch and root. Updated freshness clients route unmanaged Darwin to that same action before sending any daemon.update RPC. A migration-capable shell or TUI starts the same external preflight on ordinary Update. For already-installed binaries that cannot obtain new code before shutdown, the supported first-upgrade action is the current standard installer, including the normal Install action in an updated IDE. No manual service administration is part of that path. The distributed first-stage verifier authenticates the exact bridge commit, tree and build inputs before checkout or running any candidate-controlled instructions. Updated IDEs ship the same first-stage verifier.\",\n          \"rollbackable\": true\n        },\n        {\n          \"order\": 3,\n          \"action\": \"Verify the signed release evidence and stage the candidate in a separate owned root. Verify the real incumbent against the supported profile, pin raw incumbent and candidate bytes, and check launchd admission. A failed verification leaves the existing daemon alone. Fresh install enters the activation reservation with explicit start intent. Refresh trust at admission and persist the profile receipt before the first mutation. Offline recovery can use that admitted evidence for verified rollback.\",\n          \"rollbackable\": true\n        },\n        {\n          \"order\": 4,\n          \"action\": \"For legacy cutover, reserve and atomically replace the canonical installed checkout entry with a gate-aware dispatcher while preserving its original directory identity. Recover either side of the exchange by journaled inode evidence. Recheck for in-flight old helpers, update markers, unsupported launch routes, and mismatched job registrations. The dispatcher has no old executable or mutable checkout target. Refuse when the old launcher or writer cannot be excluded. Keep the prior checkout pinned until rollback is no longer needed.\",\n          \"rollbackable\": true\n        },\n        {\n          \"order\": 5,\n          \"action\": \"Authenticate the running predecessor over the pinned socket and observe exact exit. While the canonical launcher remains fenced, acquire the legacy-compatible run-file guard before cleanup, reconcile only the recorded publications, and retain the guard until a verified supervisor has adopted it for startup. Transfer owner evidence under the same directory identity with no unguarded interval. Remove a verified passive old job after its child exits. Apply the stored outcome policy for graceful, broker-denied, or unknown exit.\",\n          \"rollbackable\": true\n        },\n        {\n          \"order\": 6,\n          \"action\": \"Journal the provisional selector and register the supervisor. Start a child for a normal fresh install or previously running update. Verify idle for --no-start or previously stopped update. Publish the matching durable terminal record before release. An already-managed shell/TUI update uses the external transaction even when stopped, and the running managed RPC uses the detached helper and acknowledgement handshake. For the running managed route the trusted installed helper reserves and acknowledges before it starts fetch or build. Confirm reply delivery before preparation and complete qualification before shutdown.\",\n          \"rollbackable\": true\n        },\n        {\n          \"order\": 7,\n          \"action\": \"Recover interrupted activation or cutover using the matching durable outcome. Keep the canonical dispatcher fenced. Reconcile the selector and restore the previous service only through the verified private archive route, then verify exact running or stopped state. Publish the terminal rollback receipt and outcome before the dispatcher resumes guarded legacy commands. Recover an interruption after publication without relaunching the old service. Keep the archived path stable for loaded jobs and later managed retries. Uncertain ownership retains the fence. A durable success is reconciled without rollback. A running predecessor starts only with the pinned admin preload. Route its launcher root to the canonical dispatcher. Keep the obsolete update RPC disabled in both main and admin worker before and after terminal rollback. A later update uses the verified external entry point.\",\n          \"rollbackable\": true\n        }\n      ],\n      \"backwardCompat\": \"Preserve the CLI flags, configured source, IDE IPC result shapes, supported user settings, Linux behavior and historical journal decoding. Existing binaries that stop before they can fetch the migration use the current normal installer for the first upgrade. The canonical installed launcher remains a compatible entry point after migration.\",\n      \"zeroDowntime\": false,\n      \"dataRewriteRequired\": false\n    },\n    \"alternativesConsidered\": [\n      {\n        \"id\": \"a1\",\n        \"name\": \"Release-signed build verification profiles\",\n        \"oneLineSummary\": \"Use independent release evidence for portable equivalence and an exact local pin for rollback.\",\n        \"approach\": \"Publish a signed profile per tested predecessor and platform. Verify its complete runtime inventory against an independently built reference using exact hashes by default and bounded deterministic transformations only for verified build variance. Bind the original measured bytes to the cutover journal.\",\n        \"pros\": [\n          \"Supports independently built installations inside a tested profile.\",\n          \"Rejects unexplained executable changes before shutdown.\"\n        ],\n        \"cons\": [\n          \"Requires maintaining release fixtures and qualifying each native transformation.\"\n        ],\n        \"costEstimate\": \"L\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"a2\",\n        \"name\": \"Exact release fingerprints only\",\n        \"oneLineSummary\": \"Accept an inventory of byte-identical prior builds.\",\n        \"approach\": \"Publish one exact fingerprint for each independently archived prior package and continue requiring an exact installed match.\",\n        \"pros\": [\n          \"Simple executable verification.\",\n          \"Retains the existing staging pin format.\"\n        ],\n        \"cons\": [\n          \"The same commit produced different native bytes on the two observed installs.\",\n          \"Uncatalogued local builds cannot migrate automatically.\"\n        ],\n        \"costEstimate\": \"S\",\n        \"reasonRejected\": \"An exact reference fingerprint rejects separately built valid native binaries.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"a3\",\n        \"name\": \"Rebuild a rollback release from the old commit\",\n        \"oneLineSummary\": \"Use an independently verified replacement rollback build.\",\n        \"approach\": \"Fetch and rebuild the predecessor in a separate directory using pinned release inputs, then use that result as the rollback executable.\",\n        \"pros\": [\n          \"Supports a clean executable artifact without a per-install binary catalogue.\"\n        ],\n        \"cons\": [\n          \"Does not establish the original incumbent executable's provenance.\",\n          \"Replaces the exact prior executable and introduces a new rollback compatibility surface.\"\n        ],\n        \"costEstimate\": \"L\",\n        \"reasonRejected\": \"A replacement rollback build leaves original executable provenance unverified and changes rollback identity.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"chosenAlternative\": \"a1\",\n    \"openQuestions\": [],\n    \"sequenceDefinition\": {\n      \"id\": \"macos-install-cutover\",\n      \"narrative\": \"Release authority publishes evidence before the updater runs. The controller verifies the installed release and records the exact rollback pin before it fences the launcher or asks the incumbent to exit. The existing journal settles running, idle and rollback outcomes.\",\n      \"participants\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"release\",\n          \"label\": \"Release\",\n          \"description\": \"Publishes signed and tested qualification for the exact ordered release pair.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"entry\",\n          \"label\": \"Installer\",\n          \"description\": \"Stages a release through a supported entry point.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"control\",\n          \"label\": \"Controller\",\n          \"description\": \"Owns the journal and cutover fence.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"old\",\n          \"label\": \"Legacy\",\n          \"description\": \"Pinned predecessor process and job, if present.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"new\",\n          \"label\": \"Supervisor\",\n          \"description\": \"Owns the candidate daemon or verifies idle.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"child\",\n          \"label\": \"Candidate\",\n          \"description\": \"Candidate serving only after admission.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"messages\": [\n        {\n          \"from\": \"release\",\n          \"to\": \"entry\",\n          \"label\": \"Publish proof\",\n          \"description\": \"Publish only after real independent builds and the product migration matrix pass.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"from\": \"entry\",\n          \"to\": \"entry\",\n          \"label\": \"Verify bridge\",\n          \"description\": \"The bundled verifier checks trust, the exact signed Git head and its inputs before checkout or npm scripts.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"from\": \"entry\",\n          \"to\": \"control\",\n          \"label\": \"Verify prior\",\n          \"description\": \"Verify signed evidence, inventory and runtime files while the incumbent is available.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"from\": \"control\",\n          \"to\": \"control\",\n          \"label\": \"Pin prior bytes\",\n          \"description\": \"Record the local digest of all original bytes and bind it to the verified profile.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"from\": \"control\",\n          \"to\": \"control\",\n          \"label\": \"Reserve and fence\",\n          \"description\": \"Journal the entry switch and exclude older writers.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"from\": \"control\",\n          \"to\": \"old\",\n          \"label\": \"Request exit\",\n          \"description\": \"Authenticate the socket and observe exact exit.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"from\": \"old\",\n          \"to\": \"control\",\n          \"label\": \"Verified exit\",\n          \"description\": \"Keep the launcher fenced and reconcile owned publications.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"from\": \"control\",\n          \"to\": \"new\",\n          \"label\": \"Register supervisor\",\n          \"description\": \"Verify the exact supervisor and admit the requested state.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"from\": \"new\",\n          \"to\": \"child\",\n          \"label\": \"Start child\",\n          \"description\": \"Start one child if requested or previously running.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"from\": \"child\",\n          \"to\": \"new\",\n          \"label\": \"Verify readiness\",\n          \"description\": \"Verify authenticated child and publication identities.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"from\": \"new\",\n          \"to\": \"control\",\n          \"label\": \"Verify idle\",\n          \"description\": \"No child is admitted and no daemon readiness is expected.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"from\": \"control\",\n          \"to\": \"entry\",\n          \"label\": \"Commit success\",\n          \"description\": \"Persist verified running or stopped state.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"from\": \"control\",\n          \"to\": \"new\",\n          \"label\": \"Reconcile candidate\",\n          \"description\": \"On verified uncommitted failure, stop the exact candidate and remove its owned job.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"from\": \"control\",\n          \"to\": \"old\",\n          \"label\": \"Restore archived prior\",\n          \"description\": \"Keep the dispatcher fenced and start the pinned archived release with the trusted preload blocking the old update RPC.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"from\": \"control\",\n          \"to\": \"entry\",\n          \"label\": \"Publish rollback\",\n          \"description\": \"Verify prior state and publish terminal rollback before enabling guarded legacy commands. Keep the fence on uncertainty.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"companions\": [\n      {\n        \"kind\": \"diagram-mermaid\",\n        \"relPath\": \"docs/standalone/enable-managed-daemon-lifecycle-macos-install-E202611115d5478c6/S001/sequence-diagram.html\",\n        \"title\": \"Sequence diagram\"\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"citations\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c1\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"scripts/insrc-daemon-install.sh\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c2\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/shared/managed-daemon-installation.ts\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c3\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/daemon/managed-update-recovery.ts\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c4\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/daemon/managed-update-transaction.ts\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c5\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/bin/insrc-daemon-control.ts\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c6\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/daemon/legacy-launch-job.ts\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c7\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/daemon/legacy-daemon-cutover.ts\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c8\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/daemon/update-runner.ts\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c9\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/daemon/admin-handlers.ts\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c10\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/daemon/release-compatibility.ts\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c11\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/daemon/index.ts\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c12\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/shared/daemon-run-files.ts\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c13\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"scripts/daemon-ctl.sh\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c14\",\n      \"kind\": \"prior-artifact\",\n      \"ref\": \"ISSUE-5d5478c61a25ac8f\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c20\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/daemon/managed-release-staging.ts\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c21\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/daemon/managed-update-preparation.ts\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c22\",\n      \"kind\": \"prior-artifact\",\n      \"ref\": \"BUILD-5d5478c61a25ac8f-s1\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c23\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"scripts/insrc-daemon-install.sh\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c24\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \".npmrc\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c25\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/daemon/legacy-launch-job.ts\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c26\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/daemon/admin-handlers.ts\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c27\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/daemon/threads/admin-worker.ts\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c28\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/daemon/__tests__/managed-update-handoff.test.ts\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n","document":"<!-- insrc:artifact LLD-5d5478c61a25ac8f-s1 -->\n\n# LLD: STORY-5d5478c61a25ac8f-s1 — Enable managed daemon lifecycle on macOS install and upgrade\n\n## Summary\n\n**Epic:** `enable-managed-daemon-lifecycle-macos-install`\n**HLD base run:** `wf-1794513859350-cd601e`\n**HLD effective hash:** `c1ae66d7690a...`\n\nThe normal macOS installer provisions a per-user supervisor and automatically migrates supported locally built installs. Before cutover the updater verifies the incumbent against release-authorized build evidence and pins its original bytes for rollback. Existing configuration, stores and running state survive a verified rollback.\n\n## Contents\n\n1. [HLD context](#1-hld-context)\n2. [Contract details](#2-contract-details)\n3. [Data model changes](#3-data-model-changes)\n4. [Diagrams](#4-diagrams)\n5. [Error paths](#5-error-paths)\n6. [Test strategy](#6-test-strategy)\n7. [Migration](#7-migration)\n8. [Alternatives considered](#8-alternatives-considered)\n9. [References](#9-references)\n\n## 1. HLD context\n\n**Framework:** Standalone feature — no parent HLD. Design directly against the repo, grounded on the s1 analyze passes. There are no HLD shared contracts to honour.\n**Rollout phase:** standalone\n\n## 2. Contract details\n\n**Surface level:** internal-shared\n\n### 2.1 `stageManagedRelease`\n\n```typescript\nstageManagedRelease(options: StageOptions): Promise<{ readonly candidate: StagedReleasePin; readonly prior?: StagedReleasePin; readonly compatibility?: QualifiedReleasePair }>\n```\n\n**Parameters:**\n- `options: StageOptions` — Carries the configured source, exact release pair, authorized build evidence and optional incumbent.\n\n**Returns:** `Promise<{ readonly candidate: StagedReleasePin; readonly prior?: StagedReleasePin; readonly compatibility?: QualifiedReleasePair }>` — Verified candidate and optional byte-exact original rollback pin.\n\n**Errors:**\n- `Error` when Missing or invalid signed evidence, unsupported build profile, unaccounted executable differences, ambiguous inventory or unstable pin.\n\n**Preconditions:**\n- The official installer script and IDE-bundled copies carry the same dependency-free Node verifier and pinned offline release root. An installed managed controller can reuse its verified copy. Fetch into a fresh owner-private bare Git directory with external hooks, checkout filters and config injection disabled. Before checkout, npm scripts, build, or launch of a downloaded bridge, the trusted verifier validates the signed source/branch/full commit and expected Git tree, approved build inputs and release policy. A configured URL override changes routing only. Never obtain the first verifier or root from the fetched branch.\n- Use separately root-signed UTF-8 trust data, and per-channel Ed25519 qualification keys authorized by that data. Verify signatures over exact length-framed UTF-8 payload bytes with distinct trust and qualification domain tags. Enforce strict versioned schemas and limits and reject duplicate keys. Trust data records monotonic epoch, permitted source and branch per key, key ID and public key, validity window and revocations. The root is pinned in the official distribution. Root rotation requires an updated trusted distribution. Production verifier trust excludes fixture keys.\n\n**Postconditions:**\n- Refresh root-signed trust data from the configured release channel during preparation and within five minutes before the first cutover mutation. Trust data is valid for at most 24 hours. Validate the UTC window, key status and epoch against an owner-private durable floor advanced atomically under admission. If the same epoch arrives with different bytes, the local clock has rolled behind the stored valid-time floor, or signed trust data is unavailable, refuse admission. Qualification carries key ID, release source, exact current branch head and Git tree, ordered release pair, validity window, supported profile and immutable release-test receipt digests. A revoked key, expired qualification, stale trust epoch or changed candidate prevents cutover.\n- Initial profile targets the measured arm64 macOS 27.0 installation class and predecessor ab6a96296ea6f2bde948636424218c0a23136c74. Reference inputs pin Node 22.23.2 with ABI 127, npm 10.9.8, node-gyp 11.5.0, Node 22.22.1 headers, Apple Clang 21.0.0 build 2100.3.34.2, Command Line Tools 27.0.0.0.1788430756, SDK 27.0, Python 3.12.6, -std=c++17 and exact Git tree and lockfile. Release authority records digests for tools, SDK/headers, dependencies, prebuilds and environment allowlist. It produces references from two clean installer builds at separate real paths, with pinned inputs and controlled network dependency retrieval. Each supported optional dependency layout is inventoried and qualifies separately. No profile ships before the real product matrix confirms these inputs and the target incumbent.\n- Inventory exact relative paths, type, permitted modes, symlink targets, and digests for the Node runtime, the executable closure in out and node_modules, project-memory dist, scripts used by the daemon or supported entry points, and build/package configuration used by those entry points. Walk by stable owned file handles without following unapproved links. Reject extra or missing execution-bearing files, escaped links or ambiguous optional package layouts. Default to raw SHA-256. A signed profile may explicitly enumerate inert build leftovers with their expected formats and paths. Generated Makefile, gyp and dependency records, intermediate Mach-O object/static library files and npm bookkeeping are eligible only if a verified runtime or rollback path never reads or executes them. Validate their types and allowlisted formats and retain every byte in the raw pin. Optional JavaScript or WASM packages are executable and use a separately qualified exact inventory.\n- Native equivalence is limited initially to the signed exact paths of the fs-ext, tree-sitter, tree-sitter-sql and tree-sitter-kotlin thin arm64 bundles observed in the native study. Parse the entire file without gaps or overlaps. Preserve raw bytes of code, mapped data, all padding outside explicitly validated linker regions, and loader semantics including virtual addresses, dependency names, binding, export, rebase, relocation and indirect-symbol tables. Only known debug-only N_SO and N_OSO records may replace an exact observed checkout root with a token. Only N_OSO may normalize its object timestamp. Preserve record order, indices, types, sections, flags, all other values and decoded names. Verify runtime tables never reference any normalized debug record or a mutable byte of its strings. Reject all other native variations.\n- Derive the normalized representation from a checked physical layout. For each profile require the signed region order, alignment, permitted padding bytes and extent rules. Verify LC_SYMTAB addresses and sizes select its original bounded symbol and string tables. Decode and validate all n_strx references and account for the complete original string table including suffix sharing, terminators and padding. Preserve exact non-debug values and all runtime reference relationships. Construct a deterministic tokenized string table from the ordered symbol records and retained non-debug strings, with stable offsets assigned in first-appearance order. Replace only validated physical offsets, lengths and string offsets with logical region identifiers and their derived canonical offsets and lengths. Compute the normalized LC_SYMTAB strsize and __LINKEDIT filesize from these canonical extents while preserving loader-visible addresses, protections and alignments. Verify LC_CODE_SIGNATURE dataoff equals the validated original signature boundary and original CodeDirectory codeLimit equals dataoff, with complete coverage from offset zero and no unsigned gap or trailing payload. Verify the original linker-signed ad hoc CodeDirectory page hashes, expected identity, flags and absence of extra slots or entitlements. Represent signature offset and codeLimit by the same derived canonical boundary after normalization and preserve the verified signature policy in the digest. Normalize the linker UUID and generated page hashes only after those checks. Compare the versioned logical representation with the signed reference. The representation is evidence only and is never executed or written over the original binary.\n- After independent verification capture and retain a byte-exact digest of every installed runtime and allowed inert file, including portable verifier metadata. Journal the raw incumbent and candidate pins and the signed evidence digest with the exact update before fencing. Recheck raw bytes and identities on both sides of the fence and before rollback. Build the authenticated candidate with the qualified inputs, and verify its signed inventory before executing its installed controller or daemon.\n\n### 2.2 `completeLegacyMigration`\n\n```typescript\ncompleteLegacyMigration(gate: InstallationMutationGate, update: UpdateReservation, pins: ManagedUpdatePins): Promise<DaemonUpdateOutcome>\n```\n\n**Parameters:**\n- `gate: InstallationMutationGate` — Owns the cutover and recovery reservation.\n- `update: UpdateReservation` — Identifies verified predecessor, release, fence and desired state.\n- `pins: ManagedUpdatePins` — Carries the qualified prior and candidate releases.\n\n**Returns:** `Promise<DaemonUpdateOutcome>` — Verified committed cutover, verified rollback, or fenced recovery.\n\n**Errors:**\n- `SupervisorLifecycleError` when A release, launch route, exact exit, or recovery state cannot be verified.\n\n**Preconditions:**\n- Use the migration-capable installer or updater to prepare the release before invoking any shutdown path. Freeze the canonical legacy launch and checkout paths and verify old mutating helpers are absent before cutover. Verify the pinned predecessor and public socket before sending shutdown.\n\n**Postconditions:**\n- Preserve the original raw pin, job policy and stopped or running intent. Keep the canonical dispatcher fenced during candidate cleanup, selector reconciliation, predecessor launch and readiness verification, and until the rollback outcome and receipt are durable.\n- For a prior running install, the reservation owner alone may use the private, byte-pinned archived checkout. Reconstruct direct argv and cwd or a private launchd descriptor, mapping only pre-recorded checkout-contained executable/script/cwd paths from the captured inode to its archived location. Preserve UID, domain, label, argument order, runtime, state-root environment and RunAtLoad/KeepAlive policy. Verify the actual registered job and socket peer before recording readiness. A previously stopped job stays passive. Refuse ambiguous mapping and retain the fence.\n- Publish the terminal rollback outcome and verified legacy route together before enabling normal access. Keep a guarded canonical dispatcher after rollback. It can select the archived release only for the verified terminal receipt, route restart and update through the same gate, and support a later managed retry without releasing the old mutating scripts. Keep the archived location stable for the live job. Journal dispatch publication separately so interrupted settlement can reconcile without another spawn. Verify exact child or idle state and preserve a committed candidate without rollback.\n- Every restored running legacy route uses a trusted, byte-pinned rollback preload from the current installer/controller, passed as an explicit Node --import argument after scrubbing user injection options. For the signed predecessor profile the synchronous Node module hook matches the verified archived admin-handlers module URL and the pinned main/admin-worker importer URLs. It supplies wrappers for both sharedAdminHandlers and adminWorkerHandlers, retaining the original table except daemon.update. The wrapper returns an update-via-current-installer refusal before invoking any old handler, so it never writes the old marker, spawns the old helper or schedules shutdown. The restored daemon keeps this refusal after terminal rollback and throughout any later migration attempt. The runtime and archived executable keep their raw pins. Set the launcher root to the stable guarded canonical dispatcher, while the daemon entry and cwd use the verified archive. Never resolve launcher operations through the archived checkout.\n- At startup require the preload and exact hook configuration in the main thread and any admin worker, including replacements. For the pinned old router, queued admin requests can only reach a constructed wrapped table. Profile validation proves the supported startup and worker imports, default worker argument inheritance and absence of an unguarded update route. A private preload receipt identifies the verified module and invocation for main and the admin worker. Fail startup or keep recovery fenced if the wrapper cannot attest either applicable path before readiness acceptance. Test both threaded and single-threaded operation. The original legacy IPC is unavailable for updating a restored predecessor. Current shell, TUI and IDE paths use the trusted installer route.\n\n### 2.3 `stageManagedInstallation`\n\n```typescript\nstageManagedInstallation(installation: ManagedInstallation, revalidate: () => Promise<void>, legacyCutover?: boolean): Promise<void>\n```\n\n**Parameters:**\n- `installation: ManagedInstallation` — Identifies the owned state root, release, runtime and selector.\n- `revalidate: () => Promise<void>` — Verifies transaction ownership before a write.\n- `legacyCutover: boolean` _(optional)_ — Selects staging for a verified predecessor.\n\n**Returns:** `Promise<void>` — Writes the staged disabled selector and its descriptor only.\n\n**Errors:**\n- `SupervisorLifecycleError` when Selector, descriptor, or state root conflicts with the reservation.\n\n**Preconditions:**\n- The installer controller owns an activation reservation with the explicit start intent and candidate pin. It has verified the canonical root, GUI launchd domain and quiescent fresh state.\n\n**Postconditions:**\n- The installer controller journals descriptor and provisional-selector publication, exact job registration and idle handshake, and optional child readiness. Enable the selector under the reservation before controller admission. Release the reservation only after a verified idle or running result is durable. --no-start leaves an enabled selector and an idle supervisor. Recovery replays an exact matching partial pair or removes only artifacts created by the activation and restores prior absence.\n\n### 2.4 `launchManagedUpdate`\n\n```typescript\nlaunchManagedUpdate(entry: string, args: readonly string[], awaitReply: () => Promise<boolean>): Promise<{ launched: true; updateId: string }>\n```\n\n**Parameters:**\n- `entry: string` — Pins the detached controller executable.\n- `args: readonly string[]` — Carries the owned release and operation inputs.\n- `awaitReply: () => Promise<boolean>` — Confirms that the update acknowledgement was written.\n\n**Returns:** `Promise<{ launched: true; updateId: string }>` — Returns the admitted update identity while the helper owns completion.\n\n**Errors:**\n- `SupervisorLifecycleError` when An update is already active or the helper cannot qualify the candidate.\n\n**Preconditions:**\n- The already trusted installed helper reserves the update and returns its acknowledgement within the existing client deadline. Launch it without fetching, installing, building or inventory verification before the acknowledgement. Stopped updates use the local controller and preserve idle intent.\n\n**Postconditions:**\n- Begin detached preparation only after confirmation that the acknowledgement was written. If reply delivery fails, cancel the unstarted reservation and leave the incumbent running. The helper verifies qualification and stages the candidate before any launcher fence, shutdown or selector change. A lost client connection after confirmed reply does not cancel the admitted update. Preserve the existing response shape and the test for preparation longer than both transport deadlines.\n\n## 3. Data model changes\n\n### 3.1 `InstallationMutationGate activation reservation` — field-add\n\nPersist one activation reservation per state root with exact owner, candidate and explicit start intent. Journal descriptor, provisional selector, exact registration, child admission, readiness and terminal outcome with identities. Previous journals remain decodable. Activation cleanup reverses only receipted owned mutations.\n\n**Call sites:**\n- `src/shared/installation-mutation-gate.ts`\n- `src/shared/managed-daemon-installation.ts`\n- `src/bin/insrc-daemon-control.ts`\n\n### 3.2 `UpdatePredecessor` — invariant-change\n\nPersist a tagged managed, legacy-job, legacy-direct, or legacy-stopped predecessor. All variants bind canonical state root, prior release/runtime and candidate pin, selector baseline or absence, and prior running state. Managed carries supervisor generation. Legacy-job carries descriptor path/hash, verified label, UID, launchd domain, program argv, working directory and RunAtLoad policy. Running legacy-job and legacy-direct carry exact process incarnation, authenticated socket peer and pinned PID/socket identities. Legacy-direct also carries rollback argv, cwd and a release-qualified environment recipe reconstructed from the supported launcher, without persisting credentials. Legacy-stopped carries proof of no live process and either a pinned idle job or verified absence. Decode historical predecessor.legacy as legacy-job and older enabled prior without legacy as managed. Reject unknown or contradictory variants. Persist each observed exit, its drain classification, and ownership-fence evidence before candidate bootstrap. A rollback route maps only verified original checkout-relative argv/cwd paths into the archived directory. It binds a single archived release and recorded job or direct launch to the update and keeps the canonical launcher fenced until terminal evidence. The archive route pins one trusted preload, its supported old release and import/worker topology. The stable launcher root is distinct from the archived daemon cwd and entry. The old update RPC remains refused for this restoration generation.\n\n**Call sites:**\n- `src/shared/installation-mutation-gate.ts`\n- `src/daemon/managed-update-recovery.ts`\n- `src/daemon/legacy-launch-job.ts`\n- `src/daemon/legacy-daemon-cutover.ts`\n\n### 3.3 `Release qualification profile and exact rollback pin` — invariant-change\n\nThe release authority owns a signed, versioned profile for one configured source, ordered commit pair and tested build profile. A release pair can have multiple profiles. Exactly one applicable profile must verify a particular installation. An update reservation owns one verified profile receipt and one exact incumbent pin and binds both to its candidate, selector and state root. The profile verifies a complete measured inventory and accounts for variability. The local pin freezes raw bytes for rollback, and the receipt is reusable during recovery without remote network access. Historical journals retain their original verification rules. Versioned notes that lack the required portable proof cannot qualify a source-built variant. Record one immutable admission receipt per update with signing key, trust epoch and payload digest, verification time, qualification and reference digests, commit/tree pair, source, release profile, raw pins and exact update ID. Before a recorded fence, expiry, refresh failure or revocation forces refusal. After the fence, the recorded decision applies only to that transaction while its owner settles it within its admitted authorization window. If recovery begins without a durable success, it can verify and roll back offline using the receipt and raw pin. It needs fresh authorization to newly activate a candidate. If revocation is known before a new candidate activation, roll back. Durable success is reconciled offline and is never reinterpreted as uncommitted.\n\n**Call sites:**\n- `src/daemon/managed-release-staging.ts`\n- `src/daemon/managed-update-preparation.ts`\n- `src/daemon/release-compatibility.ts`\n- `src/daemon/managed-update-recovery.ts`\n\n## 4. Diagrams\n\n- [Sequence diagram](docs/standalone/enable-managed-daemon-lifecycle-macos-install-E202611115d5478c6/S001/sequence-diagram.html)\n\n## 5. Error paths\n\n**Error cases**\n\n- **An older launcher or updater can still mutate the legacy install** (recoverable)\n  - Detection: Resolve the canonical checkout and every supported launch route. Atomically exchange the canonical checkout entry with a gate-aware dispatcher on the same filesystem while the old daemon is still running. Preserve the displaced checkout separately. Before stopping, verify the dispatcher and archived checkout identities, an absent or owned legacy update marker, and absence of known old shell, detached helper and installer processes bound to the retired root, using verified argv and cwd. Repeat admission after the swap. An unrecognized launch path, unresolved process identity, live old mutator, or exchange failure makes exclusivity unproven.\n  - Response: The dispatcher rejects concurrent mutation while cutover owns the reservation. Qualify only predecessors that acquire the shared run-file guard before opening data and whose shipped old commands use the canonical absolute entry. Keep that guard through owned cleanup and transfer it to candidate startup before the legacy route can reacquire it. Refuse when another legacy route cannot be fenced. Preserve or restore the old service before releasing a failed admission.\n  - User impact: The supported update retries safely after a concurrent operation ends.\n- **Predecessor job or runtime does not match the qualified route** (recoverable)\n  - Detection: Match the verified launchd registration against its owned descriptor bytes and approved exact argv. Match direct socket peer and executable against the qualified launch recipe. Accept the documented recovery LaunchAgent only with its pinned tsx loader arguments, canonical entry, matching working directory, bounded owned descriptor and passive restart policy.\n  - Response: Reject foreign jobs, unknown loaders or args, conflicting restart policy and unverifiable direct rollback settings before disruption. Recheck the descriptor, PID incarnation and run-file identities immediately before each action.\n  - User impact: The updater reports the unsupported predecessor while preserving the current service.\n- **Exit is unconfirmed or graceful drain cannot be classified** (recoverable)\n  - Detection: Observe the exact predecessor exit independently of the broker and bind any qualified terminal evidence to the authenticated shutdown request and process incarnation. An RPC acknowledgement, socket close, PID disappearance, or generic log entry alone does not prove a graceful drain.\n  - Response: Withhold candidate admission until exact exit is proved. Journal the exit plus graceful, broker-denied, or unknown. Use graceful only for a qualified positive completion signal, broker-denied only for correlated watchdog evidence, and unknown otherwise. Both broker-denied and unknown require a release pair explicitly qualified for crash-consistent reopening. Report the observed classification in the update detail.\n  - User impact: The user can distinguish a verified shutdown from an exit whose drain could not be confirmed.\n- **Candidate bootstrap or readiness fails before a durable outcome** (recoverable)\n  - Detection: Registration, startup generation, owned run files, or candidate idle/readiness cannot be verified.\n  - Response: Reconcile the candidate under the reservation, Keep the canonical dispatcher fenced while restoring the predecessor via the archived route. Publish verified rollback before enabling guarded ordinary commands. Retain a fence when restoration is unverified.\n  - User impact: Verified rollback restores the previous service state and reports the failed update.\n- **Outcome publication throws or its acknowledgement is lost** (recoverable)\n  - Detection: Publication fails, or retry sees an active reservation without a verified terminal reply.\n  - Response: Keep the reservation. Re-read the exact matching durable outcome through existing recovery. A durable success verifies or completes activation without rollback. A durable rollback verifies the old state. Only a proven uncommitted transaction can unwind. Unknown ownership remains fenced.\n  - User impact: The updater reports an unconfirmed result until recovery determines the terminal state.\n- **Release qualification is missing, ambiguous, stale, revoked or invalid** (recoverable)\n  - Detection: Reject absent note, invalid signature or key, unsupported profile or verifier version, source or commit mismatch, conflicting profiles and any unaccounted runtime difference.\n  - Response: Report the failed verification before any incumbent shutdown, fence or selector mutation. Discard only owned preparation artifacts and preserve the incumbent.\n  - User impact: The daemon remains available with an update diagnostic and can retry after a qualified release is published.\n- **Installed runtime changes after independent verification** (recoverable)\n  - Detection: Recheck the original raw digest and exact file identities while holding update admission at the designated cutover and rollback boundaries.\n  - Response: Stop cutover before touching the incumbent when possible. After mutation use the existing exact rollback or fenced recovery. Refuse to execute any changed rollback copy.\n  - User impact: The update fails with a verification diagnostic.\n- **Legacy mutation competes with rollback or cutover authorization expires** (recoverable)\n  - Detection: Read the admitted trust receipt and launcher fence identities. Detect a concurrent old command through the guarded canonical route or a fresh candidate admission after expiry or revocation.\n  - Response: Reject the old command. Use only the private archive route for rollback, verify the prior state, publish terminal evidence, then permit guarded dispatch. If admission is no longer valid, recover offline by rollback unless the candidate already has a verified durable success.\n  - User impact: The previous verified service is restored or the installation remains fenced for recovery.\n- **A restored legacy client attempts daemon.update or the rollback preload cannot bind the old handler** (recoverable)\n  - Detection: The pinned adapter wraps both legacy admin table factories and records the receipt for each applicable execution path. Refuse before the original RPC handler. Reject a startup without proven coverage.\n  - Response: Keep the legacy update RPC disabled before and after settlement. Use the guarded current installer path for a subsequent update. Retain fenced recovery if no verified restored service can be admitted.\n  - User impact: The previous service remains available through supported commands or the controller reports fenced recovery.\n\n**Edge cases**\n\n| Input | Expected |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| Fresh --no-start | Register the supervisor, enable the selector and verify idle with no daemon child. Subsequent normal start uses the same supervisor. |\n| Already-managed update when the daemon is stopped | The external controller prepares and commits the update using supervisor status; no daemon RPC is required. Keep the candidate idle. |\n| Legacy daemon remains running while the candidate is staged | Build outside the live root, preserve the running predecessor and only then acquire a cutover reservation. Failed fetch, build, or qualification leaves the running install unchanged. |\n| An unchanged older shell or IDE freshness client invokes legacy restart or daemon.update | An old client cannot acquire new preflight behavior before executing its existing shutdown sequence. The first upgrade is delivered by the current standard installer or the ordinary update action in updated IDE clients. The installer may be rerun on an existing installation without launchctl or file edits. Never present an old acknowledgement as migration success. |\n| Two qualified installs share a commit but have different build paths or raw native artifact bytes | Each matches independent expected execution-bearing bytes under the same tested profile and retains its own raw exact pin. |\n\n**Invariants to preserve**\n\n- One state root has at most one daemon process admitted to use its stores. [[c2]]\n- Committed success is never rolled back because the publication reply was lost. [[c4]]\n- Linux keeps its current lifecycle and update behavior. [[c1]]\n\n## 6. Test strategy\n\n**Test framework:** `Node.js test runner via tsx, installed shell integration fixtures, and real macOS launchd smoke tests in disposable state roots.`\n\n**Test levels**\n\n- **unit** — Verify admission, qualification, journal compatibility and outcome recovery.\n  - Subjects: `Predecessor variants and backward decoding`, `Explicit fresh activation start intent and owned partial publication`, `Release provenance, changed bytes and absent legacy declaration`, `Graceful, broker-denied and unknown drain evidence`, `Uncertain publication with durable success versus no outcome`\n- **integration** — Drive supported entry points and competing legacy operations through the real controller boundaries.\n  - Subjects: `Installer and updated CLI, VS Code and JetBrains route matrix`, `Running managed update IPC and stopped local managed update`, `Old shell/helper concurrent with canonical launcher exchange and old child attempting to open stores`, `Startup guard transfer, candidate bootstrap failure, rollback and repeated recovery`, `Linux normal install, update and restart unchanged`\n- **smoke** — Verify actual launchd and daemon state on macOS.\n  - Subjects: `Fresh install and verified ordinary restart`, `Fresh --no-start and later supervisor start`, `First supported update of a qualified prior build with broker denial`, `Direct install and passive recovery job with exact loader argv`, `Verified stopped and running rollback with configuration and store integrity`\n  - Fixtures: `Disposable macOS state roots and isolated launchd labels`, `Pinned released predecessors, including the regression and clean-drain cases`\n- **contract** — Verify release signatures, independent expected inventory, applicability, exact runtime coverage, and report provenance.\n  - Subjects: `Independent same-commit macOS build equivalence and tamper rejection`, `Changed native code, loadable data, bindings, signature policy, injected modules, symlinks and unidentified variance fail closed`, `Missing or stale release evidence prevents promotion and leaves the incumbent running`\n- **smoke** — Require real product builds before promoting a source-built profile.\n  - Subjects: `Release publication validates real running and stopped upgrades for every enabled profile`, `Real product passive and recovery job loader arguments, verified broker refusal, data-preserving rollback and subsequent managed update`\n  - Fixtures: `Two independent production installer builds of the same supported predecessor in different homes with observed native variance`, `Real predecessor and candidate installs using disposable homes and distinct launchd jobs`, `Qualification from an isolated release authority, plus immutable test receipts bound to promoted release metadata`\n- **integration** — Prove trust ordering and rollback fences at the real controller boundaries.\n  - Subjects: `Trust rotation, expiry, replay and offline rollback at the fence boundary`, `Invalid or missing authorization prevents bootstrap checkout execution, npm scripts and bridge invocation`, `A competing legacy command and interruption during rollback cannot reopen the launcher before durable outcome`\n  - Fixtures: `A trusted installer and distinct trusted/unauthorized signed Git origins with executable marker probes`, `A real archived legacy daemon, fresh and expired signed trust epochs, and controller crash points before outcome and before guarded dispatch`\n- **contract** — Cover derived Mach-O layout, signature coverage and preserved runtime semantics.\n  - Subjects: `Independent unequal-length debug roots preserve canonical native identity and malformed offsets or codeLimit are rejected`\n  - Fixtures: `Two real fs-ext builds with different path lengths and different physical string-table size, __LINKEDIT extent, signature offset and codeLimit`, `Malformed linkedit offsets, unexpected string aliases or padding, truncated CodeDirectory and mismatched codeLimit`\n- **integration** — Prove restored old IPC containment and preserve the bounded managed acknowledgement.\n  - Subjects: `Real restored legacy update RPC cannot mutate the marker, run the old helper or stop the daemon during rollback and managed retry`, `Managed acknowledgement precedes a preparation exceeding the client deadlines and lost reply cancels only unstarted work`\n  - Fixtures: `Actual old predecessor with normal and threaded admin routing and worker replacement, paused before rollback publication and during a later managed preparation`, `Existing managed-update-handoff slow preparation fixture, Unix socket delivery failure and correlated update outcome`\n\n**Acceptance mapping**\n\n| Criterion | Proving tests |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| `ac1` | `Fresh install and verified ordinary restart`, `Fresh --no-start and later supervisor start` |\n| `ac2` | `Installer and updated CLI, VS Code and JetBrains route matrix`, `First supported update of a qualified prior build with broker denial`, `Direct install and passive recovery job with exact loader argv`, `Old shell/helper concurrent with canonical launcher exchange and old child attempting to open stores` |\n| `ac3` | `Running managed update IPC and stopped local managed update`, `Verified stopped and running rollback with configuration and store integrity`, `Uncertain publication with durable success versus no outcome`, `Linux normal install, update and restart unchanged` |\n| `ac4` | `Independent same-commit macOS build equivalence and tamper rejection`, `Release publication validates real running and stopped upgrades for every enabled profile`, `Trust rotation, expiry, replay and offline rollback at the fence boundary`, `Invalid or missing authorization prevents bootstrap checkout execution, npm scripts and bridge invocation`, `A competing legacy command and interruption during rollback cannot reopen the launcher before durable outcome`, `Independent unequal-length debug roots preserve canonical native identity and malformed offsets or codeLimit are rejected`, `Real restored legacy update RPC cannot mutate the marker, run the old helper or stop the daemon during rollback and managed retry`, `Managed acknowledgement precedes a preparation exceeding the client deadlines and lost reply cancels only unstarted work` |\n\n## 7. Migration\n\n**State before:** The s1 discovery bundle identified whole-tree prior fingerprint qualification before managed migration. Two builds of the same legacy commit produced different native executable bytes. The configured origin was missing the qualification ref.\n\n**State after:** Current install and update entry points share a migration-capable controller and immutable releases. A supported first upgrade retains the previous release and establishes one verified per-user supervisor. User data remains in the existing state root.\n\n**Zero downtime:** no — **Data rewrite:** no\n\n**Steps**\n\n1. The release maintainer builds independent references, signs profile and product test receipt digests, and uploads qualification for the exact target commit. A promotion gate verifies the note and reference receipts against the intended head before advancing the channel ref. Publish the note before the branch moves, or publish both atomically. Re-fetch both from the ordinary updater endpoint and confirm the signed head and trust epoch agree. Unsupported profiles remain unavailable. Fixture keys never authorize production. — ↩ rollbackable\n2. Use the current standard installer for both fresh installs and first upgrades. Updated VS Code and JetBrains lifecycle Update on unmanaged Darwin dispatch the packaged migration-capable installer in existing-install mode with the configured branch and root. Updated freshness clients route unmanaged Darwin to that same action before sending any daemon.update RPC. A migration-capable shell or TUI starts the same external preflight on ordinary Update. For already-installed binaries that cannot obtain new code before shutdown, the supported first-upgrade action is the current standard installer, including the normal Install action in an updated IDE. No manual service administration is part of that path. The distributed first-stage verifier authenticates the exact bridge commit, tree and build inputs before checkout or running any candidate-controlled instructions. Updated IDEs ship the same first-stage verifier. — ↩ rollbackable\n3. Verify the signed release evidence and stage the candidate in a separate owned root. Verify the real incumbent against the supported profile, pin raw incumbent and candidate bytes, and check launchd admission. A failed verification leaves the existing daemon alone. Fresh install enters the activation reservation with explicit start intent. Refresh trust at admission and persist the profile receipt before the first mutation. Offline recovery can use that admitted evidence for verified rollback. — ↩ rollbackable\n4. For legacy cutover, reserve and atomically replace the canonical installed checkout entry with a gate-aware dispatcher while preserving its original directory identity. Recover either side of the exchange by journaled inode evidence. Recheck for in-flight old helpers, update markers, unsupported launch routes, and mismatched job registrations. The dispatcher has no old executable or mutable checkout target. Refuse when the old launcher or writer cannot be excluded. Keep the prior checkout pinned until rollback is no longer needed. — ↩ rollbackable\n5. Authenticate the running predecessor over the pinned socket and observe exact exit. While the canonical launcher remains fenced, acquire the legacy-compatible run-file guard before cleanup, reconcile only the recorded publications, and retain the guard until a verified supervisor has adopted it for startup. Transfer owner evidence under the same directory identity with no unguarded interval. Remove a verified passive old job after its child exits. Apply the stored outcome policy for graceful, broker-denied, or unknown exit. — ↩ rollbackable\n6. Journal the provisional selector and register the supervisor. Start a child for a normal fresh install or previously running update. Verify idle for --no-start or previously stopped update. Publish the matching durable terminal record before release. An already-managed shell/TUI update uses the external transaction even when stopped, and the running managed RPC uses the detached helper and acknowledgement handshake. For the running managed route the trusted installed helper reserves and acknowledges before it starts fetch or build. Confirm reply delivery before preparation and complete qualification before shutdown. — ↩ rollbackable\n7. Recover interrupted activation or cutover using the matching durable outcome. Keep the canonical dispatcher fenced. Reconcile the selector and restore the previous service only through the verified private archive route, then verify exact running or stopped state. Publish the terminal rollback receipt and outcome before the dispatcher resumes guarded legacy commands. Recover an interruption after publication without relaunching the old service. Keep the archived path stable for loaded jobs and later managed retries. Uncertain ownership retains the fence. A durable success is reconciled without rollback. A running predecessor starts only with the pinned admin preload. Route its launcher root to the canonical dispatcher. Keep the obsolete update RPC disabled in both main and admin worker before and after terminal rollback. A later update uses the verified external entry point. — ↩ rollbackable\n\n**Backward compat:** Preserve the CLI flags, configured source, IDE IPC result shapes, supported user settings, Linux behavior and historical journal decoding. Existing binaries that stop before they can fetch the migration use the current normal installer for the first upgrade. The canonical installed launcher remains a compatible entry point after migration.\n\n## 8. Alternatives considered\n\n### 8.1 a1: Release-signed build verification profiles — **CHOSEN**\n\nUse independent release evidence for portable equivalence and an exact local pin for rollback.\n\nPublish a signed profile per tested predecessor and platform. Verify its complete runtime inventory against an independently built reference using exact hashes by default and bounded deterministic transformations only for verified build variance. Bind the original measured bytes to the cutover journal.\n\n### 8.2 a2: Exact release fingerprints only\n\nAccept an inventory of byte-identical prior builds.\n\nPublish one exact fingerprint for each independently archived prior package and continue requiring an exact installed match.\n\n**Rejected because:** An exact reference fingerprint rejects separately built valid native binaries.\n\n### 8.3 a3: Rebuild a rollback release from the old commit\n\nUse an independently verified replacement rollback build.\n\nFetch and rebuild the predecessor in a separate directory using pinned release inputs, then use that result as the rollback executable.\n\n**Rejected because:** A replacement rollback build leaves original executable provenance unverified and changes rollback identity.\n\n## 9. References\n\n- **[[c1]]** `code` `scripts/insrc-daemon-install.sh`\n- **[[c2]]** `code` `src/shared/managed-daemon-installation.ts`\n- **[[c3]]** `code` `src/daemon/managed-update-recovery.ts`\n- **[[c4]]** `code` `src/daemon/managed-update-transaction.ts`\n- **[[c5]]** `code` `src/bin/insrc-daemon-control.ts`\n- **[[c6]]** `code` `src/daemon/legacy-launch-job.ts`\n- **[[c7]]** `code` `src/daemon/legacy-daemon-cutover.ts`\n- **[[c8]]** `code` `src/daemon/update-runner.ts`\n- **[[c9]]** `code` `src/daemon/admin-handlers.ts`\n- **[[c10]]** `code` `src/daemon/release-compatibility.ts`\n- **[[c11]]** `code` `src/daemon/index.ts`\n- **[[c12]]** `code` `src/shared/daemon-run-files.ts`\n- **[[c13]]** `code` `scripts/daemon-ctl.sh`\n- **[[c14]]** `prior-artifact` `ISSUE-5d5478c61a25ac8f`\n- **[[c20]]** `code` `src/daemon/managed-release-staging.ts`\n- **[[c21]]** `code` `src/daemon/managed-update-preparation.ts`\n- **[[c22]]** `prior-artifact` `BUILD-5d5478c61a25ac8f-s1`\n- **[[c23]]** `code` `scripts/insrc-daemon-install.sh`\n- **[[c24]]** `code` `.npmrc`\n- **[[c25]]** `code` `src/daemon/legacy-launch-job.ts`\n- **[[c26]]** `code` `src/daemon/admin-handlers.ts`\n- **[[c27]]** `code` `src/daemon/threads/admin-worker.ts`\n- **[[c28]]** `code` `src/daemon/__tests__/managed-update-handoff.test.ts`\n"},"capturedAt":"2026-11-12T20:04:35.381Z"},"inventory":{"units":[{"id":"/body/hldContextSlice","selector":"/body/hldContextSlice","title":"hldContextSlice","kind":"section"},{"id":"/body/summary","selector":"/body/summary","title":"summary","kind":"section"},{"id":"/body/contractDetails","selector":"/body/contractDetails","title":"contractDetails","kind":"section"},{"id":"/body/dataModelChanges","selector":"/body/dataModelChanges","title":"dataModelChanges","kind":"section"},{"id":"/body/interactionWithShared","selector":"/body/interactionWithShared","title":"interactionWithShared","kind":"section"},{"id":"/body/errorPaths","selector":"/body/errorPaths","title":"errorPaths","kind":"section"},{"id":"/body/testStrategy","selector":"/body/testStrategy","title":"testStrategy","kind":"section"},{"id":"/body/migration","selector":"/body/migration","title":"migration","kind":"section"},{"id":"/body/alternativesConsidered","selector":"/body/alternativesConsidered","title":"alternativesConsidered","kind":"section"},{"id":"/body/chosenAlternative","selector":"/body/chosenAlternative